WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.634
-Michael Strahan is joining us!

00:00:02.634 --> 00:00:05.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:07.868
The number 92 in New York
is retired.

00:00:07.868 --> 00:00:11.634
And -- That is big -- That's
big-enough news to talk about.

00:00:11.634 --> 00:00:12.968
-Yeah.

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:14.567
-But then you announced
that you're going to space.

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:15.968
-I'm going to space, man.

00:00:15.968 --> 00:00:17.634
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-That's right.

00:00:17.634 --> 00:00:19.834
With Jeff Bezos' Blue Origin.

00:00:19.834 --> 00:00:21.901
You're -- You're going to space.

00:00:21.901 --> 00:00:24.567
-I'm going to space,
December 9th.

00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:27.701
And I'm glad you gave me a joke
from your monologue,

00:00:27.701 --> 00:00:29.901
actually, that you didn't use.
-I would never --

00:00:29.901 --> 00:00:31.567
I would never use it,
but I wanted

00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:32.968
to give it to you just --
-Yeah, you gave it to me.

00:00:32.968 --> 00:00:35.300
I want -- Y'all mind if I read
what he was going to say?

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.367
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-Here we go.

00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:41.000
"Now, when you google
'Strahan in space,'

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.367
you don't just get
a picture of his smile."

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:45.934
-[ Laughter and applause ]
-That's not bad.

00:00:45.934 --> 00:00:48.734
That's a good joke.
-You know what?

00:00:48.734 --> 00:00:52.367
So I googled -- I googled
"Jimmy Fallon in space."

00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:53.667
You know what came up?
-What?

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:54.801
-Virgin.

00:00:54.801 --> 00:00:58.200
[ Laughter ]

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.501
-Yeah.

00:00:59.501 --> 00:01:01.100
-It's all good, man.
It's all good.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.367
-Talk to me about this.
Talk to me about Blue --

00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:05.467
How do you --
How do you team up with Bezos?

00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:07.300
What is going on?
What does your family think?

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.200
What -- Well, let's just --
How do you team up?

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.734
-Well, it's crazy. Blue Origin.
Big Blue, the Giants.

00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:14.701
It's just kind of a marriage
made in heaven, I guess.

00:01:14.701 --> 00:01:17.100
But I went to the first launch.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.968
And I watched Jeff
and his brother, Mark,

00:01:18.968 --> 00:01:20.434
and the other crew members
go up,

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:22.033
and I remember
being there thinking,

00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:24.501
"I'll never --
No way I would ever do it."

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:26.501
It was the most amazing thing

00:01:26.501 --> 00:01:28.667
I've ever seen
outside of childbirth.

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:31.200
And it would --
made you emotional to see it

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.133
and it completely
changed my mind

00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:37.033
about why, if I would do it,
the reasoning behind it,

00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:38.801
why other people want to do it.

00:01:38.801 --> 00:01:43.667
And -- And then I pretty much,
a few months later or so,

00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:46.801
got a call saying,
"Do you want to go up?"

00:01:46.801 --> 00:01:47.968
-Really?

00:01:47.968 --> 00:01:49.634
-And I had expressed
to some people that,

00:01:49.634 --> 00:01:52.701
"Hey, I would actually do that,"
after seeing it.

00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:54.801
And apparently they heard it,
and they called me,

00:01:54.801 --> 00:01:58.067
and I had stuck my foot too deep
in my mouth to pull it out.

00:01:58.067 --> 00:01:59.734
-I mean, you --

00:01:59.734 --> 00:02:01.534
What was the part
that freaked you out?

00:02:01.534 --> 00:02:02.868
Was it the launching?

00:02:02.868 --> 00:02:04.701
Or was it the coming --
-All of it was really --

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:06.901
Like, when the rocket comes
back down, it was like Ironman.

00:02:06.901 --> 00:02:09.267
This thing comes back
and lands on the pad.

00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:11.767
-And things come out
and legs come out,

00:02:11.767 --> 00:02:13.200
and it's -- Yes.
-Yeah.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.667
And then the capsule, like,
this -- You -- You're looking.

00:02:15.667 --> 00:02:17.267
You're going, "Okay.
I'm going to see them floating."

00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:19.000
But then they disappear.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.634
And then you go,
"Ooh, that's really high."

00:02:20.634 --> 00:02:22.601
-Wow.
-And I was --

00:02:22.601 --> 00:02:24.267
I had to fly, you know,
the other day.

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:25.868
And I'm, like,
looking at the thing,

00:02:25.868 --> 00:02:27.501
and it tells you
you're 43,000 feet.

00:02:27.501 --> 00:02:29.300
And I'm going,
"Man, this is way up here.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.434
Look at this."

00:02:30.434 --> 00:02:31.634
Then I realize, this is nothing.

00:02:31.634 --> 00:02:34.901
So I might wear a diaper.

00:02:34.901 --> 00:02:36.467
-Yeah, I mean -- No. Yeah.

00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:39.834
How do you -- But how do you --
How do you physically prepare?

00:02:39.834 --> 00:02:41.167
How do you mentally prepare?

00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:43.267
-Well, physically,
you go there and you train.

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:45.868
I think the -- the toughest
thing they say is really

00:02:45.868 --> 00:02:48.868
just trying to get yourself
in and out of the seatbelt.

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:50.734
So, it's like
a five-point harness.

00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:52.234
Bop, bop, bop, like a racecar.

00:02:52.234 --> 00:02:53.534
-Uh-huh.
-And I'm a car guy.

00:02:53.534 --> 00:02:56.067
So I have cars with that.
-So you know that.

00:02:56.067 --> 00:02:58.234
-Yeah, so I'm kind of
cool with that.

00:02:58.234 --> 00:02:59.567
I fit in the seat easily.

00:02:59.567 --> 00:03:01.234
I was worried about that.
-Yeah.

00:03:01.234 --> 00:03:02.801
-But I fit in the seat
pretty easily.

00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:04.334
And just the way
it's all engineered.

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:05.901
And when you talk
to these astronauts

00:03:05.901 --> 00:03:08.334
who have been a part
of this program for so long,

00:03:08.334 --> 00:03:10.033
who have been astronauts
for 30-plus years,

00:03:10.033 --> 00:03:11.601
who have been a part
of Blue Origin

00:03:11.601 --> 00:03:13.501
for 10-plus, 20 years.
-Smart people.

00:03:13.501 --> 00:03:15.501
-It's the smartest people
in the world.

00:03:15.501 --> 00:03:17.634
They -- You feel so comfortable.

00:03:17.634 --> 00:03:19.501
And I feel safer doing this
I think

00:03:19.501 --> 00:03:22.300
than I do getting on a plane
to fly to L.A.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.000
every weekend to do
my Fox football show.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.434
-Really?
-Because there's not some,

00:03:25.434 --> 00:03:27.634
like, guy in air-traffic control
who is stressed out, you know?

00:03:27.634 --> 00:03:29.300
These are, like,
people who are focused

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:30.801
directly on that and only that.

00:03:30.801 --> 00:03:33.200
-But are you talking
to the fellow passengers?

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.434
Are you talking --
Did you talk to William Shatner?

00:03:35.434 --> 00:03:37.000
-I haven't talked
to William Shatner yet.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.567
I have not talked to him yet.
I've --

00:03:38.567 --> 00:03:41.100
I've been in communication with
the people I'm going up with.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.601
I've talked to Jeff Bezos.

00:03:42.601 --> 00:03:44.968
I've talked to -- had a chance
to talk to Mark Bezos about it.

00:03:44.968 --> 00:03:47.033
And the description
of it is like --

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:49.234
Even the release
of the capsule from the rocket.

00:03:49.234 --> 00:03:51.367
Like, it's really not
as dramatic as you think.

00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:53.467
I mean, we're used to Tom Hanks
in "Apollo 13."

00:03:53.467 --> 00:03:54.634
[ Imitating rumbling ]

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:56.334
It's -- It's not like --
They're like, "No."

00:03:56.334 --> 00:03:57.934
It's like the ship
is designed to go up.

00:03:57.934 --> 00:03:59.968
And as it's going up, it turns.

00:03:59.968 --> 00:04:02.934
And you look out the window,
so you can just see everything.

00:04:02.934 --> 00:04:04.267
It's really cool.

00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:05.501
-I want to --
I want to -- I want to know.

00:04:05.501 --> 00:04:06.834
Like, I want to talk to you
as soon as you --

00:04:06.834 --> 00:04:09.100
'Cause, I mean, William Shatner
did it, came down and --

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.334
And everyone was expecting him
to be like, "Oh, yeah.

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:12.968
I'm Captain Kirk."
And blah, blah, blah.

00:04:12.968 --> 00:04:14.601
-Uh-huh.
-He was emotional.

00:04:14.601 --> 00:04:16.033
He couldn't talk.

00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:19.701
And he said, "What I just saw
was, like, life and death."

00:04:19.701 --> 00:04:21.567
-Well, I hope
that ain't the case, but...

00:04:21.567 --> 00:04:24.501
-Okay. Maybe don't talk
to William Shatner.

00:04:24.501 --> 00:04:26.934
-I just want to see
the life side.

00:04:26.934 --> 00:04:28.467
-Don't talk to
William Shatner yet.

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:31.667
But -- But he said, like --
It was like -- It was deep.

00:04:31.667 --> 00:04:33.300
It was like -- You saw light.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.133
And then you go like, "Oh, yeah.
We'll go out of the atmosphere."

00:04:35.133 --> 00:04:36.767
And then for a second,
you're just like --

00:04:36.767 --> 00:04:38.567
Everything is just dark

00:04:38.567 --> 00:04:40.367
and you're weightless
and you're floating.

00:04:40.367 --> 00:04:41.601
And you're like, "What is this?"

00:04:41.601 --> 00:04:43.534
And then you come back
to reality.

00:04:43.534 --> 00:04:45.634
And just everything
in life becomes --

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:47.601
It already is precious,
as we know,

00:04:47.601 --> 00:04:49.701
but you really think about it
and you go, "Wow."

00:04:49.701 --> 00:04:51.300
-It gives you
a different perspective.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.200
And I think that's what
I'm looking forward to,

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.801
to see a different perspective
on -- you know,

00:04:55.801 --> 00:04:58.033
almost like an out-of-body
experience of looking

00:04:58.033 --> 00:05:01.067
at planet Earth where we live
from outside of planet Earth...

00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:02.834
-Yeah.

00:05:02.834 --> 00:05:05.267
-...and kind of understanding
the bigger picture of --

00:05:05.267 --> 00:05:07.434
of the planet and a bigger
picture of why we're here.

00:05:07.434 --> 00:05:09.834
And, for me, to be on --
on "GMA" every morning,

00:05:09.834 --> 00:05:13.334
but to be able to come in
and express that to people

00:05:13.334 --> 00:05:14.934
from someone
that they see every morning

00:05:14.934 --> 00:05:16.801
and to have the opportunity
to do that.

00:05:16.801 --> 00:05:18.767
And not just someone who goes up
and they come down

00:05:18.767 --> 00:05:20.234
and they do a thing
here and there,

00:05:20.234 --> 00:05:22.834
but to be there every day
being able to explain this.

00:05:22.834 --> 00:05:26.067
And so that has my curiously
piqued to do it.

00:05:26.067 --> 00:05:27.868
-Wow. We love you, man.
Be safe and --

00:05:27.868 --> 00:05:30.067
-I will. I'll be safe.
-We'll be watching.

00:05:30.067 --> 00:05:31.634
-When I'm back, I'm coming here
to talk about it.

00:05:31.634 --> 00:05:35.567
-Yeah. Michael Strahan,
everybody. We love him.

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:37.534
His clothing line,
"Michael Strahan,"

00:05:37.534 --> 00:05:39.100
is available at Men's Wearhouse,

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.634
JCPenney stores nationwide,
and on MichaelStrahanBrand.com.

